To draw a chessboard with a given FEN (Forsyth-Edwards Notation) state in LaTeX, you can use the `chessboard` package and the TikZ (TikZ and PGF) graphics system. Here is a LaTeX code that includes the necessary commands and packages:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{chessboard}
\usepackage{tikz}

\begin{document}

% Define the FEN state
\newcommand{\fenstate}{rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq -}

% Use the chessboard environment to draw the board
\newcbboardsetup{showmover=false} % Optional: hide the mover (optional)
\begin{cbboard}[setup=newcbboardsetup]
\fenboard{\fenstate}
\end{cbboard}

% Draw the board using TikZ
\begin{tikzpicture}
\node[draw, minimum size=3cm, thick] (board) at (0,0) {};
\foreach \x in {0,1,...,7} {
    \foreach \y in {0,1,...,7} {
        \ifnum \fenstate[\x*\y] = 1
            \node[circle, inner sep=0.8pt, fill=black] (square\y\x) at (\x,\y) {};
        \fi
    }
}
\end{tikzpicture}

\end{document}
```

This code uses the `chessboard` package to define the FEN state and draw the board, and the `tikz` package to draw the pieces using circles. The `newcbboardsetup` defines the options for the `cbboard` environment, allowing you to customize the appearance of the board. The FEN state is stored in the `\fenstate` command and used in the `\fenboard` command to set the initial board position. The `tikzpicture` environment draws the individual chessboard squares and places black and white pieces accordingly.

